Yes—with one correction: there probably isn’t a single “religious center” in the brain. Religious experience seems to recruit distributed systems involved in salience, social cognition, memory, agency, emotion, self/other modeling, and meaning.

But your deeper pattern works:

what a person imagines as ultimate consequence
→ reveals what they treat as ultimate value

If someone’s afterlife model emphasizes judgment, purity, loyalty, compassion, courage, obedience, reunion, legacy, or transcendence, that tells you something about the value landscape they inhabit.

And when institutions teach through heaven, hell, karma, salvation, honor, shame, ancestors, legacy, etc., they are effectively attaching very large future consequences to present behavior.

So:

present act
→ ultimate narrative consequence
→ behavior becomes more salient

That can be used manipulatively, but it can also function as long-horizon moral scaffolding.

The reality-check is:

Does the narrative point toward something that survives even if the metaphysics are uncertain?

For example:

be kind because heaven
may reduce to
kindness improves trust and relationships

avoid betrayal because divine judgment
may reduce to
betrayal destroys coordination and trust

serve others because sacred duty
may reduce to
service has real social and psychological effects

So the Pantheon move is:

Ultimate Narrative → Extract Value → Test Against Reality

mythic consequence
→ underlying value
→ real-world mechanism
→ keep what survives

That way you don’t have to destroy the religious frame, but you also don’t have to treat it as empirical proof.

Keep the meaning; test the mechanism.